<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/the-novel-a-short-history">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The novel is <strong>about four hundred years old and now the world's dominant literary
    form</strong> — written in every major language, read at every level of society, the
    container for most of the stories modern people actually know. Its history is not a list
    of great books but a sequence of <strong>technical inventions</strong>: free indirect
    style, the serial cliffhanger, stream of consciousness, the unreliable narrator. Each one
    expanded what prose could hold, and each one is still on the syllabus — from Austen's
    Pride and Prejudice to Ayanna Lloyd Banwo's When We Were Birds on the Cambridge set list.
  </p>
  <p>
    Knowing the history changes how you read, because every novel is doing something with
    the devices its era invented. Emma is unimaginable without free indirect style; Mrs
    Dalloway is unimaginable with it. This lesson runs the story forward, then zooms in on
    the two skills the exam actually tests: reading plot shape and tension, and reading a
    whole novel as a designed object.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four centuries of invention, from Quixote to magical realism" widget="equationStep" config={novelCfg} />

  <h2>Before and at the start: Don Quixote</h2>
  <p>
    Long prose fiction existed before 1600 — Greek romances, Lady Murasaki's Tale of Genji
    (early eleventh century Japan, arguably the first psychological novel), the sprawling
    French and Spanish romances of chivalry. What Cervantes did in Don Quixote (part one
    1605, part two 1615) was different in kind: he wrote a <em>parody</em> of romance in
    which a man who has read too many romances rides out to live them. The joke has a
    consequence. Once the hero mistakes windmills for giants, every episode must run on two
    levels at once — the ideal as imagined and the real as it bites — and the novel as a
    form was born in that gap: ordinary people, unglamorous particulars, an interior life at
    odds with the world, and a narrator who knows more than his characters. Part two even
    has the characters hear about part one — self-awareness that postmodernists would
    recognise as their own.
  </p>

  <h2>The eighteenth century: the form finds its market</h2>
  <p>
    Why England, why then? The historian Ian Watt's <em>The Rise of the Novel</em> (1957)
    gives the classic answer: a rising middle class with money for books and leisure to
    read; cheap print and circulating libraries; and a Protestant culture of private
    self-examination that made the individual conscience a fit subject for long prose. The
    early novels answered that demand. Defoe's Robinson Crusoe (1719) faked a memoir so
    convincingly that many first readers took it for fact — realism arriving as
    verisimilitude of document. Richardson's Pamela (1740) told its story in letters —
    epistolary form, psychology written from inside the moment. Fielding's Tom Jones (1749)
    replied with an omniscient, ironic narrator who chats with the reader while conducting
    a meticulously plotted comedy. Within thirty years the form had its three permanent
    narrative positions: first person, letters, and the god's-eye ironist.
  </p>

  <h2>The nineteenth century: the great engines</h2>
  <p>
    The nineteenth century industrialised the form, and its key invention was
    <strong>free indirect style</strong>: third-person narration that slides into a
    character's own vocabulary and judgements without quotation marks. Watch the opening of
    Austen's Emma: "Emma Woodhouse, handsome, clever, and rich… seemed to unite some of the
    best blessings of existence" — and then, almost unnoticed, "The real evils, indeed, of
    Emma's situation were the power of having rather too much her own way." The praise is
    Emma's own self-image leaking through the narrator's grammar; the irony needs no
    commentary because the syntax delivers it. This device let the classic novel report a
    mind and judge it in the same breath, and it runs from Austen through Flaubert to
    almost every third-person novel on a modern shelf.
  </p>
  <p>
    The century's other engine was economic. Dickens published in monthly parts
    (The Pickwick Papers, 1836–37, began the fashion), each nineteen-page instalment ending
    on a hook, each instalment priced for a mass readership. The serial mode built the
    cliffhanger into the novel's skeleton decades before television, and it shaped
    structure at every scale: a plot that must re-grip its reader every month grows
    subplots, recurrence and coincidence. Meanwhile in Russia, Tolstoy and Dostoevsky
    stretched the form toward philosophy and theology — the novel as the era's moral
    laboratory, a place where ideas could be lived rather than merely stated.
  </p>

  <h2>Plot, narrative shape and tension</h2>
  <p>
    Now the analytical toolkit, because Paper 1 Section B examines whole prose texts. Start
    with E. M. Forster's famous distinction in <em>Aspects of the Novel</em> (1927): "The
    king died and then the queen died" is a story — a sequence. "The king died, and then the
    queen died of grief" is a plot — a sequence <em>with causality</em>. Everything the
    novel does with plot is an elaboration of that one word <em>of</em>. The reader's
    engagement runs on two interlocking questions — what happens next, and why did that
    happen — and writers manage them differently: mystery novels foreground
    <em>why</em> (withhold the cause), thrillers foreground <em>what</em> (withhold the
    event), literary novels often subordinate both to character revelation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shape is how the causal chain is bent. The classical arc — opening situation,
    destabilising event, rising complication, climax, resolution — is a scaffold, not a
    law: nineteenth-century panoramic novels braid several arcs through subplots;
    epics-in-prose like Middlemarch run four protagonists' arcs in counterpoint so that
    each marriage is measured against the others. The arc's name comes from Gustav
    Freytag's 1863 study of drama, and it describes a five-act inheritance the novel kept
    and bent: Austen's Pride and Prejudice destabilises in its first scene (a rich stranger
    insults its heroine in public), climbs through two failed proposals, turns at Darcy's
    letter — the novel's hinge, where the reader's information flips — and resolves by
    re-running the first proposal under changed knowledge. Tension is managed by
    <strong>delay</strong> (the promised confrontation postponed), <strong>foreshadowing</strong>
    (a planted detail that gathers meaning later), <strong>dramatic irony</strong> (the
    reader knows what the character does not), and <strong>pacing shifts</strong> — a scene
    dramatised minute by minute against a summary that leaps a decade. When you analyse a
    set novel, find where it destabilises, where it turns, and what it delays: that map
    <em>is</em> the structure essay.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> plot is not "what the book is about" — that conflation
  flattens analysis into retelling. Plot is one designed layer among several; examiners
  repeatedly penalise narrative summary that never asks how the narrative is built. Write
  about the architect, not the furniture.</p>

  <h2>The twentieth century and after: the form explodes</h2>
  <p>
    Modernism broke the container on purpose. Joyce's Ulysses (1922) and Woolf's Mrs
    Dalloway (1925) pursued <strong>stream of consciousness</strong> — rendering thought as
    it actually flows, associative and unpunctuated; Woolf's party proceeds by way of its
    attendees' drifting minds, and Joyce's final chapter gives Molly Bloom a closing
    soliloquy of roughly twenty thousand words in eight enormous sentences. The innovation
    answered a question the nineteenth century left open: can prose narrate consciousness
    itself, not just behaviour? Meanwhile the <strong>unreliable narrator</strong> matured
    — narrators who mislead or lie (Nabokov's Humbert, Ishiguro's butler in The Remains of
    the Day), making the reader a diagnostician who reads against the text.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the form went global and plural. Magical realism (Márquez's One Hundred Years of
    Solitude, 1967) treated the marvellous as reportage; postcolonial novels (Achebe's
    Things Fall Apart, 1958; Rushdie's Midnight's Children, 1981) seized the form the
    empire brought and turned it on the empire; Borges and Calvino made the novel
    self-questioning. The current set list makes the point concretely: Ayanna Lloyd
    Banwo's When We Were Birds (2022), set in Trinidad, brings Caribbean myth and
    graveyard magic into the same lineage Austen and Dickens built — proof that the form
    absorbs everything and keeps growing, now in every language on Earth.
  </p>

  <h2>Voice: who is speaking, and can you trust them?</h2>
  <p>
    Between plot and style sits the question examiners reward most: <strong>who is telling
    this, and what does their position do to the truth?</strong> First-person narrators are
    characters — they have limits, motives and blind spots. Henry James built whole novels
    on a single limited consciousness; Ishiguro's Stevens narrates The Remains of the Day
    in polished butler prose that lies by omission, and the reader must hear the cracks in
    the diction ("dignity", "professionalism" repeated like a mantra). The
    <strong>unreliable narrator</strong> is not a trick but a contract: the text plants
    enough evidence for you to read against the teller, and the gap between story and
    telling becomes the novel's real subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    Austen's Pride and Prejudice shows the omniscient version of the same game: the opening
    sentence — "It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of
    a good fortune, must be in want of a wife" — sounds like a law of nature, and by the
    end of the paragraph the narrator's irony has flipped it: the "universally
    acknowledged" truth is really the neighbourhood mothers' wishful thinking. Free
    indirect style then lets the irony move inside Elizabeth's head. When you read a set
    novel, label the narrative position early (first person? ironic third? epistolary?
    multiple focalisers?) and write your essay from inside that choice — it governs
    everything else, including what the plot is allowed to show you.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Watt's "rise of the novel" thesis is the standard account, and also the standard
    target. Margaret Anne Doody's <em>The True Story of the Novel</em> (1996) argued the
    form is far older — ancient Greek and Roman prose fictions were novels avant la lettre
    — so 1719 is a story about <em>England</em>, not about the form. Watt's defenders reply
    that something genuinely new happened around 1700: formal realism, the systematic
    preference for particular, verifiable, middling-life detail over romance generality.
    Both can be true: the novel as human practice is ancient; the novel as a dominant,
    realist, middle-class institution is modern. Exam boards love the distinction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two technical refinements finish the toolkit. First, the narrative theorist Gérard
    Genette separated the questions students blur: <strong>focalisation</strong> (whose
    consciousness the narration passes through) is not the same as narration (who speaks) —
    a novel can speak in third person while seeing through one pair of eyes, and noticing
    the split is often the whole reading. Second, narrative <strong>duration</strong> —
    scene time versus summary time — is quantitative: measure pages against days and you
    have a graph of what the novelist thinks matters. When Márquez spends a page on one
    execution and a sentence on seven decades, the ratio is the interpretation.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the economics never stopped shaping the form. The circulating libraries that fed
    the eighteenth-century boom rented novels in three volumes (publishers priced to
    make the triple-decker profitable), which is why Victorian novels run so long;
    instalment fiction gave way to magazine serialisation, then to the paperback (Penguin,
    1935, sixpence — the novel in every pocket), then to the book club and the
    translation market that now launches a novel in thirty languages simultaneously. The
    lesson is general: every era's novel carries its media in its shape, from monthly
    cliffhangers to the swipe-length chapters of contemporary fiction. Reading the form's
    history and reading its publishing history are, in the end, the same activity.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Watt, The Rise of the Novel (1957); Forster, Aspects of the Novel (1927); Genette, Narrative Discourse (1972); Doody, The True Story of the Novel (1996); the primary novels themselves.</p>
